                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[h1]A Labor of Love…[/h1]\n\nDead Estate is a roguelike about shooting your way through a haunted mansion while a giant mutant named Chunks tries to enforce the property’s no-trespassing policy.\n\nIt’s extremely good.\n\nThe shooting feels great, movement is tight, and the different characters actually change how you play instead of just having slightly different numbers attached to them. There’s a ridiculous amount of weapons, items, secrets, and other shit to mess around with, and most of it is genuinely worth finding.\n\nIt’s also one of those games where you can come back after six months and immediately start playing again. I couldn’t tell you what half these items do anymore, but apparently my hands have been maintaining a separate relationship with Dead Estate behind my back.\n\n[b]The amount of content is fucking absurd.[/b]\n\nMilkbar Lads put out six major free updates over three years, adding characters, weapons, floors, bosses, endings, and an entire separate campaign. Most developers would’ve split this shit into three DLCs and made you buy a deluxe edition that comes with a digital art book nobody’s ever opened.\n\nThese guys apparently just kept making more game. I don’t know what their financial situation looks like, but I hope somebody’s checking on them.\n\nThe pixel art is excellent, the music rules, and the whole thing has that old Newgrounds sensibility I miss. It’s weird, a little horny, occasionally disgusting, and completely comfortable with itself. The people making it clearly knew what they were doing, which makes some of their creative decisions considerably more concerning.\n\nMy only real complaints are that the isometric perspective occasionally makes aiming and judging jumps a little awkward, and some of the rooms start feeling pretty familiar after a while. Neither is a particularly big deal.\n\nI really don’t have much else to bitch about. It’s fun immediately, gets better the more you play, and somehow has more content than games with significantly larger budgets and entire departments dedicated to explaining why they couldn’t finish theirs.\n\nMilkbar Lads made a great game and then spent three years giving people more of it for free. Pretty cool of them. Financially concerning, but cool.",
